[h1]D'thorga Radio Framework[/h1]
[b]Version:[/b] 0.55.0
[b]Publication status:[/b] Not published yet - draft held for the owner's review

[h2]Read this first (gotchas)[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Not yet tested in game.[/b] It builds and installs, but its buildings, its feedline network, its buttons and its mood have not been seen running in a game.
[*][b]Work on this mod is paused for now.[/b] No new features are planned until it resumes; a fault that is reported is still fixed.
[*][b]Needs D'thorga Framework and Vanilla Expanded Framework.[/b] Vanilla Expanded Framework's pipe network carries the radio feedline, and D'thorga Framework gives the upgrades. Load this mod after the game, its expansions, Vanilla Expanded Framework and D'thorga Framework. The mod asks for that order itself, so RimSort sorts it for you.
[*][b]The upgrades are experimental:[/b] the radio desk, the whip and the mast each have an Upgrades button.
[*][b]RimWorld 1.7 only.[/b]
[*][b]Decide before you start a colony:[/b] the mod setting Metres per cell (default 1, from 0.5 to 30) changes how far every signal and every feedline reaches. Changing it in a running colony changes which radios can reach each other. The “In touch” mood can be turned off in the same settings.
[*][b]The hand-held radio is worn on the belt[/b], so a colonist cannot wear a shield belt and a hand-held at the same time.
[*][b]Safe to add to a running colony.[/b]
[*][b]Safe to remove once you have deconstructed every radio building and dropped every hand-held.[/b] The first load afterwards shows one harmless “could not find class” error.
[/list]

[h2]What it does[/h2]
Radio that works like radio. Whether two radios can reach each other is worked out from real physics: how strong the transmitter is, the antennas, the cable to them, the distance, what stands in the way, and the electrical noise your own colony makes. Twice the distance gives a quarter of the signal.

This first part covers short-range radio on one map. Research Local radio (after Microelectronics Basics), then build:
[list]
[*][b]Hand-held radios[/b], VHF or UHF, made at the machining table and worn on the belt. VHF reaches further outdoors; UHF copes better with walls.
[*][b]A radio base station[/b] (Architect > D'thorga > Radio), which needs power and has a short antenna of its own.
[*][b]Coax feedline[/b], laid like power conduit from the station to an antenna. It loses some signal along its length and can run under walls.
[*][b]A whip antenna[/b] (3 m high), a cheap way to get the antenna outside, or [b]a mast antenna[/b] (10 m high), which sees over walls.
[*][b]Upgrades[/b], fitted by builders: a quieter receiver for the desk, a taller pole for the whip, and an extension or a longer antenna for the mast.
[/list]

What weakens a signal: distance; walls and closed doors on the straight line between two antennas, where that line is below 2.5 m (wood least, plasteel most); natural rock, heavily; the roof over each end; a long feedline; and your own generators and batteries, which add noise near them.

Colonists who can reach home by radio get an “In touch” mood. Three display modes show as much of the working as you like: Plain shows signal bars and a reason in plain words, Operator adds the radio figures, and Engineer shows every step of the sum. The result is the same in each.

Everything is a Def, so another mod can add a radio, a band, an antenna or a noisy machine without any code.

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[list]
[*]RimWorld 1.7
[*][url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2023507013]Vanilla Expanded Framework[/url]
[*]D'thorga Framework
[/list]

